# home.filter: what archive-sync leaves out of a cluster home when that
# backend has no filter of its own. It leaves out what can be installed,
# rebuilt or downloaded again, and keeps everything else.
#
# To change it for one backend, copy it to
# ~/.config/cluster/archive/<backend>-home.filter (fasrc-home.filter, say) and
# edit the copy; archive-sync uses the copy from then on.
# `archive-sync --show-config` says which filter is in use.
#
# The syntax is rclone's: the first rule that matches a path wins, "- "
# excludes and "+ " includes, a leading / anchors a rule at the top of the
# home, and dir/** matches a directory and everything in it. Only whole lines
# are comments: text after a rule becomes part of its pattern.
#
# Symlinks that lead out of the home (into scratch or project space) need no
# rule here: archive-sync fences them on every run. A directory that holds a
# file named .nosync is left out too, with everything in it.
#
# Version-control directories (.git) are kept: they can hold commits and
# branches that exist nowhere else. If every repository in the home is pushed
# somewhere, a "- .git/**" line in your copy makes the archive much smaller.

# Conda and Python installations and environments
- /.conda/**
- /miniconda3/**
- /anaconda3/**
- /miniforge3/**
- /mambaforge/**
- /.local/lib/**
- .venv/**
- venv/**
- .tox/**

# JavaScript packages
- /.npm/**
- /.nvm/**
- node_modules/**

# Caches
- /.cache/**
- __pycache__/**
- .pytest_cache/**
- .mypy_cache/**
- .ipynb_checkpoints/**

# Editor servers, installed again on the next connection
- /.vscode-server/**

# Editor swap files
- *.swp
